<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Today Is Tuesday! – Lesson # 4</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Tuesday!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write their own name and other very familiar words without assistance. Interactive: Draw pictures related to a simple sentence they have written.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write their name and days of the week without assistance (e.g., "Yamileth" and "Tuesday"). Can write simple greetings on cards (e.g., "Happy Monday!"). Can draw pictures for sentences they have written (e.g., a greeting card for the sentence "Happy Monday!").</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large flashcards of the days of the week one by one. Students choral repeat the names of the days. The teacher then asks, "What day is today?" while pointing to the "Tuesday" flashcard. Students respond "Today is Tuesday!". The teacher draws a simple happy face on the board and says, "Happy Tuesday!".</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher says, "My name is [Teacher's Name]." and slowly writes their name clearly on the whiteboard. Then, the teacher says, "Today is Tuesday." and writes "Tuesday" below their name, emphasizing the letter 'T' and 'd' sounds /t/ and /d/ while writing. The teacher demonstrates how to write "Happy Tuesday!" on a small card and draws a simple sun, modeling clear letter formation for 1st graders.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to "Tuesday" on the board: "Is this Monday or Tuesday?" (Expected: "Tuesday!")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to their written name: "Is this 'book' or 'my name'?" (Expected: "My name!")</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All Week Long!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, simple visual calendar showing all days of the week with an empty space under each day. Flashcards with simple greetings like "Happy Monday!", "Happy Tuesday!", "Happy Wednesday!" paired with matching cheerful drawings. A simple template of a greeting card.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Using time-related vocabulary: Identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them. Sociolinguistic: Using polite expressions when talking about the days of the week.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to the calendar and says, "Today is Tuesday!" and asks, "What day is this?" while pointing to a different day flashcard (e.g., "Monday"). Students point to the corresponding day on the calendar.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard with "Happy Friday!" and a drawing. "Is this for Monday or Friday?" Students point to the correct written day.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Grammar: Noun + verb + noun (e.g., "Today is Tuesday.").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, day, today.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation: Phonemic awareness of initial sounds /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day".</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Days of the Week Tracing":</b> The teacher provides worksheets with the names of the days of the week (Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday...) written in dotted lines. Students trace each day's name. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pencil grip or letter formation.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Fill-in-the-Day":</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with simple sentences like "Today is ______." and a word bank of the days of the week. Students look at the day flashcard displayed by the teacher and write the correct day in the blank. Example: Teacher shows "Tuesday" flashcard, students write "Tuesday".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Practice: "My Name and a Day":</b> Students receive a small paper strip. The teacher instructs them to first write their own name clearly. Then, they choose one day of the week they like (e.g., "Saturday") from the board and write it below their name. In pairs, students show their paper strips to each other and try to read their partner's name and the day they wrote.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All Week Long!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will create a "Happy Day!" greeting card for a friend or family member, focusing on writing their name and a simple greeting with a day of the week, and drawing a related picture.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a folded piece of paper to make a simple card.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reminds students of the model "Happy Tuesday!" and their own name.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are instructed to first write their own name inside the card.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next, they choose one of the days of the week they have learned (e.g., Monday, Tuesday, Friday, weekend) and write a simple greeting like "Happy Monday!" or "Happy weekend!" on the front of the card.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, students draw a simple picture on the card that matches their chosen greeting (e.g., a sun for "Happy Monday!", a relaxing picture for "Happy weekend!").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students share their finished cards with a partner, pointing to their name and the day they wrote.</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "Happy Day!" greeting cards. The teacher observes their writing process and reviews the final product.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes own name legibly (without assistance).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes a day of the week (e.g., "Tuesday") legibly.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to write a simple greeting (e.g., "Happy Monday!").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Draws a picture related to the written sentence/day.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to use target vocabulary (days of the week, happy).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write my name: Yes / No" (Students circle or check).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 'Tuesday': Yes / No" (Students circle or check).</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one word you learned to write today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you draw on your card?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The tracing and fill-in-the-blank activities helped students gain confidence in writing the days of the week. Most students were able to write their names and at least one day correctly. Some struggled with letter formation for longer words like "Wednesday." Next time, I could provide more visual guides for letter strokes or practice specific tricky letters (e.g., 'W', 'd'). Scaffolding for the next lesson (Mediation & Integration) will involve reviewing the written greetings and using them to discuss simple daily plans or schedules.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's writing practice with names and days of the week will prepare students for Lesson 5, where they will use these words to complete a simple weekly schedule or make a class calendar as part of a 21st Century Skills Project, involving simple mediation tasks like pointing and matching written items to pictures or actions.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>